{
  "dom_id": "step-q65573",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Given that the expression $2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x+\u03d5)$ with $R>0$, what is the value of $R?$",
  "worked_solution": "The sum formula for sine gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x+\u03d5) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)+R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)=R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x+\u03d5),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5)}^{2} & ={2}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}\u2061\u03d5+{sin}^{2}\u2061\u03d5) & =4+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =20 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$",
